now it's been a successful commonwealth  games for england this year currently  second in the medal tally with more than  40 goals but  one athlete who's really stood out is  freestyle swimmer tom dean who recently  became england's most decorated athlete  at a single commonwealth games winning  a total of well he's here tom dean is  here tell us how many it is well i can  see them yeah so it's seven medals all  in uh six silvers and one gold medal  well congratulations look at that hall  it practically takes up the whole sofa  absolutely how does it feel to have  made this extraordinary record no it's  incredible and it was about halfway  through my week of racing where  um i started to ask a question actually  how many is the record  and then we did a little bit of digging  and it came out there was six was the  most decorated english um athlete across  all sports uh for total meadow hall and  and i realized i had seven races all in  so if everything went to plan and you  know the stars aligned then then i'd be  able to hit that magical seven and you  know it's still slightly you know  slightly overwhelmed still now you know  even when you just read out there it's  absolutely incredible  so even though you knew it was possible  was it ever really in your expectations  do you think um it was always in the  back of my mind i didn't want to jinx it  though you know i didn't want to say it  too early um but it was always in the  back of my mind and the thing is there's  so many variables you know everything  has to go perfectly the races have to be  executed perfectly and when i'm doing  the relays you know you're relying on  three other people to execute the best  race to get that medal so  a lot of things had to go right and  luckily they did and particularly sweet  the last one was the gold the last one  was the gold yet so six silvers and i  was chasing that gold the whole time and  a few of those silvers were really  really close races and and finally you  know to win on that relay and i was i  was doing the final leg of the relay the  anchor and it was just the perfect  ending to the week oh very exciting and  tell us a little bit about your your  rivalry with duncan scott i mean  how does that affect your performance do  you think is it actually helpful to you  do you think um i can't seem to get away  from the guy honestly um no i think we  didn't invite him today yeah true true  the first day i've got away from him um  no it's it it is great we're really  great mates you know in and out of the  pool but that rivalry is is you know so  so tight um so my my medley uh swim for  example i think he had me by twelve one  hundredths or thirteen one hundredths of  a second so you know it's so so close  fine fine margins but i think we do push  each other on as well you know um  because we do similar events both in the  same country you know we were one and  two at the olympics last year in the 200  freestyle so we're always you know  getting the best out of each other and i  mean it's an extraordinary result after  what i'm sure has been a lot of build up  and training and hard work give us a  sense of what is involved in preparing  for games like this yes it's a year-long  preparation ultimately so we finished  the olympics last year i took a small  break and then it's straight back in you  think about the next one so it is a full  you know 11 months of training and  that's 10 swim sessions a week four gym  sessions a week we're doing core we're  doing you know stretching sessions in  the round it's you know 30 plus hours a  week of training and that's for 11  straight months so  finally when you get to competition  that's almost the best bit because  you've done the hard work and you can  just focus on the racing but then it  becomes about executing those perfect  race plans you know doing exactly what  you've been working on in training yeah  and and making all that training count i  guess and not not um wasting that  opportunity  and so what now do you get a rest now do  you get to kind of long lines and not go  near a swimming pool that would have  been nice but actually i was back in the  water uh yesterday training so we we did  the last race on wednesday and then i  was back in the the training hub in bath  back back to training because we've got  the europeans next week so we fly out to  rome on monday so it is very much back  to back and and we're straight back into  another block of racing but then luckily  i get a break before we start the next  season and is that a good or a bad thing  having them so close together do you  have a sort of momentum do you feel  going from one event on to another so  quickly yeah absolutely and especially  when you've got good results behind you  you know i know i do well out in uh up  in birmingham so i know i can just carry  that good form on to to rome um when you  have a slightly longer break you know it  you have to kind of train and then and  then come back down and rest back up but  with this you just bounce straight on  and you just straight back into the next  one you just carry those you know your  sharpness and like your race readiness  into the next one well that's  interesting because i mean how much is  it about  feeling physically tired or not and what  about mentally tired as well i mean is  it quite difficult to be that focused  over so many events um in different  places absolutely it is tough and and  one thing that um i think sometimes  swimming fans  or swimming spectators don't quite  understand is even though you're adding  one extra race that means you're adding  a heat a semi and a final so three more  races you've got to get up for so even  though i did you know seven um seven  medals that's like 11 or 12 swims all in  so you're having to get yourself up for  it physically emotionally you get to the  end of the week it's just like you're  completely drained so it is quite  demanding and that's why i think you  know having a big break after europeans  is something that's really important and  something that people have started to  wake up to the fact that it can take in  a bit of a mental toll as well  so and so you will take a break oh yeah  i will i can't wait i've got uh yeah a  good four weeks off and i'll be able to  switch off you know have those long  lines get away from the pool for a  little bit because you know ultimately  we're two years away from paris and it's  going to be a tough two years of  training and you know that's the big  focus  of course that's that's so that's coming  up relatively soon isn't it absolutely  and what about your reaction back home i  think your your mum threw a rather  famous 3am party didn't she when you  were competing in tokyo you won the gold  medal that was it yep absolutely and  that that that's legendary  how have they reacted this time so  luckily they were actually able to come  up to birmingham to watch me this time  which was really special no yeah 3m  garden parties with me on the other side  of the world um you know and then  watching on a big the big projector  screen yeah they're able to come up  which is great i saw them after every  race which was absolutely brilliant and  you know when i get back from europeans  we're going to have a big celebration  big big summer party and you know i'll  be able to have all my family and  friends they'll be really special so  what difference has it made now that  we're out of a pandemi which which must  have played havoc with a lot of the  competitions yeah the competition time  with training as well you know the big  impacts on our training um  birmingham crowd i was just saying  earlier i've never ever experienced  anything like it it was unreal five  thousand fans in the sandowell aquatic  center all on their feet you know  whether it was like a heat session on  day three or the big final session on  day six they were just absolutely  bouncing so when i won that relay got my  seventh medal you know reached you know  the most decorated english athlete and  one that you know the the final race the  whole me it was just absolutely electric  i've never ever experienced anything  like it and why do you think that was do  you think it's partly because everyone  was so happy to be back watching  competition again abs 100 you know been  starved of live sport for two years and  and you know swimming especially it's  just it's just not the same without that  crowd so everyone's come back you know  home games to to win at the end it was  just you know the support for the  english swimmers was incredible well  huge congratulations it may take you  some time to pick up all your medals  and leave the studio but uh thank you  for bringing them in and uh what an  achievement tom dean really loved to see  you thanks very much

